A||||9||Shiner||Another frickin problem||||11||
Z||000000||Shiner||09-08-2000||01:05 AM||||Here goes.  My restoration project ('87 GT) needs a lot.  One of the things she needs is a nose.  I purchased an '86 SE 2M6 parts car for a very good price.  The nose is structurally intact, with only paint chipping and flaking in the clear coat.  The problem with this is that part of the trim is molded into the nose, aka "I have the frickin round trim, damnit, and this nose doesn't"  With that said, do you guys think there is any way I could modify this to look at least SOMEWHAT like the round trim on the rest of my car?  I am putting a bunch of money into this GT, and I really don't want to have an almost perfect car with mistmatched trim.<P> Suggestions?<P>------------------<BR>Shiner-<BR>'84 Sport Coupe<BR>'87 GT<BR>'86 SE 2M6 (parts car)<BR>"Stupidity should hurt"||||reg||11
Z||000001||fropuff||09-08-2000||09:59 PM||||Shiner:  I am not an expert in ANY way, so don't listen to me unless someone else says my idea will work.  I was thinking that you would be able to fill in the grooves with a filler to make it smooth.  Such as Bondo.  Then, you could work with it until it looks good.  Bondo might not stick unless the plastic is roughed up???  Then, you could buy some flexible trim/bumper paint and paint all the molding at the same time.  That way, all of it will match.  Anyone else think this will work?  I just wanted to give Shiner an idea, since nobody else has.  Again, I am NOT an expert, so don't try my suggestion until someone confirms that it will work.  Just giving you an idea.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shiner||09-09-2000||02:54 AM||||Fropuff,<P> Thanks for the suggestion.  I was thinking about what I could use to fill it and sand it smooth, but I don't know of anything that would work with the darn urethane bumper. <P> Anybody?<P> Or how about a trade?  I could throw in the SE nose and some other parts maybe?||||reg||1
Z||000003||wla21841||09-09-2000||08:48 AM||||Right out of the factory service manual. Use a polyester body filler. Ihaven't checked on fillers yet but my 85SE needs the trim on the front and rear filled do to the sun down her in Florida. the top of the moldings are eaten away. Also the book said to use 3M 8101 for rapair and filling. I got some here from a local body paint supply for $15.00. Haines manual said to use 5900 but that is not available but the 8101 is.<P>Bill Adamson||||reg||1
Z||000004||contra||09-09-2000||03:46 PM||||i would advise against body filler to be honest<BR>i bondo'd my front bumper addon onto my car, made it all look stock, and nice<BR>and then my painter said "no no no no no" and pushed it lightly with his thumb, and it all cracked and crumbled out.<P>so we fiberglassed the whole thing. I would suggect fiberglassing the modling, or body fill it, then fiberglass overtop and sand. it'll last longer, and it's plastic attaching to plastic which will work better.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||09-09-2000||06:04 PM||||Use some urethane bumper repair epoxy. I think bondo sells it. The stuff is like gas tank repair epoxy and I have used it to fix a ripped grand am bumper before.<P>You might want to just post in the mall section and ask if anyone wants to trade.||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||09-09-2000||08:06 PM||||Don't waste your time filling in the moldings with body filler. It will look like **** . Go to the fiero store and purchase new round moldings, there cheap enough! <P>You can also find some used in a junkyard somewhere or buy some from Kick Hill Farm.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||09-09-2000||09:24 PM||||I agree with 88Formula.....these moldings are less than $10 new, so why waste the time and effort turning the ribbed ones smooth?<P>Good luck with your project.<P>   ||||reg||10
Z||000008||Phil||09-09-2000||09:37 PM||||remember guys the front facia molding is not removable.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Shiner||09-10-2000||02:10 AM||||Phil,<P> Right on.  The very tip of the molding, way in the front is removable, but not the part to the AFT of the light marker.  <P> The Fiero Store's front bumper is $400 plus shipping.  I really don't feel like spending that much money, but if I have to.... ;(<P> I was wary about using body filler, thanks for the input guys.||||reg||1
